sinit+ - tiny init
==================
sinit+ is an improved sinit (suckless.org): a minimal init for Linux and BSD
in about 340 lines of C. It runs rc.init at boot, reaps zombies, and runs
rc.shutdown on poweroff and reboot. Everything extra is an optional module
that is compiled in only if you enable it.

An old sinit config.h works unchanged; all modules are then off.


What is new compared to sinit
-----------------------------
Core fixes (always on):
 - sigtimedwait() instead of alarm()+sigwait(); no unchecked return values
 - fds 0, 1, 2 are always open (/dev/console, else /dev/null)
 - PATH is set if the kernel did not pass one
 - SIGTERM, SIGUSR2 and SIGPWR are handled too (busybox compatible)
 - a hung rc.shutdown is cut off after SHUTDOWN_TIMEO seconds
 - repeated shutdown requests are ignored while one is running

Modules (config.h, 1 = on):
 MOD_NATIVE_SHUTDOWN  after rc.shutdown: SIGTERM, SIGKILL, sync, reboot(2)
 MOD_CAD              Linux: Ctrl-Alt-Del -> SIGINT instead of hard reset
 MOD_RESCUE           run a rescue shell if rc.init fails
 MOD_RESPAWN          keep services (getty, ...) alive, rate limited


Signals
-------
 SIGUSR1, SIGUSR2, SIGPWR   poweroff   (rc.shutdown poweroff)
 SIGTERM, SIGINT            reboot     (rc.shutdown reboot)
 SIGCHLD                    reap children

Examples:
	kill -USR2 1     # poweroff
	kill -TERM 1     # reboot


Requirements
------------
A C99 compiler and a POSIX system. MOD_NATIVE_SHUTDOWN and MOD_CAD need
<sys/reboot.h> (Linux, BSD). Build with musl for a ~20 KB static binary.


Installation
------------
Edit config.def.h, copy it to config.h if you want a custom one, then:

	make
	make install            # installs to /usr/local/sbin

Test it first (see below), then boot it with the kernel parameter
init=/usr/local/sbin/sinit. Keep your old init as a fallback entry in the
bootloader until you are sure that rc.init and rc.shutdown work.


Testing (safe, no VM needed)
----------------------------
	make test

This builds sinit+ with test/config.h and runs it as PID 1 inside its own
PID namespace, with demo rc.init / rc.shutdown scripts. It walks through the
whole life cycle: rc.init, a respawned service, SIGTERM, rc.shutdown, the
native kill/sync step and reboot(2). reboot(2) inside a PID namespace only
ends that namespace, so the real system is never touched (and sinit refuses
to run unless it is PID 1 anyway).

Needs a C compiler and util-linux "unshare". Runs as root, or as a normal
user if unprivileged user namespaces are enabled. On Ubuntu 24.04 and newer
they may be restricted; then run it with sudo.

Manual mode: SINIT_TEST_MANUAL=1 make test, then from another terminal:
	kill -USR2 $(pgrep -x sinit)     # poweroff, exit status 130
	kill -TERM $(pgrep -x sinit)     # reboot,   exit status 129


Notes
-----
 - MOD_RESPAWN services start only after rc.init has exited. If your rc.init
   stays running, they will not start.
 - rc.init and rc.shutdown are plain scripts you write yourself; see
   config.def.h for the default paths.
 - Manual: man ./sinit.8


